        "status": "source",
        "sourceId": "eng-t113-v2",
        "html": "<p>The word <b>Sabbath</b> means to rest or to stop. The Sabbath is the seventh day of the week for the <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"eng-t259-v2\" data-resource-code=\"FIAKeyTerms\">Israelites</data>, or \"Jews.\" It is a day to rest and <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"eng-t149-v1\" data-resource-code=\"FIAKeyTerms\">worship</data> God. On that day, the Jews often gather together in the <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"eng-t133-v1\" data-resource-code=\"FIAKeyTerms\">synagogue</data>, which is the local Jewish meeting place, to <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"eng-t102-v1\" data-resource-code=\"FIAKeyTerms\">pray</data> and listen to people teach about <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"eng-t189-v1\" data-resource-code=\"FIAKeyTerms\">God</data>. Because festival days were days to rest as well, people often called the festival days \"Sabbaths,\" even if the festival day was not on the seventh day of the week.</p><p>The <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"eng-t77-v2\" data-resource-code=\"FIAKeyTerms\">Jews</data> counted days from evening to evening. That means that the Sabbath began on the evening of the sixth day and finished on the evening of the seventh day.</p><p>In the time of Moses, God had commanded the Israelites to rest from all their work on each seventh day. God told the people that God had created the world in six days and rested on the seventh day. For that reason, the seventh day was a <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"eng-t67-v1\" data-resource-code=\"FIAKeyTerms\">holy</data> day. The seventh day belonged to God. When people rested each seventh day, people learned that they could trust God for all their needs. People did not need to be working all the time because God was taking care of the people. This day of rest was a beautiful gift of God to the people. The Israelites needed to keep this rest throughout the whole year, even during the busy time of the harvest. When the Israelites kept this day of rest, people from other countries could see that the Israelites were different. It was a sign that the Israelites were God's special people.</p><p>It was very important to God that the Israelites did the right things on the Sabbath. If someone <em>did</em> work on the Sabbath, we say that that person did not \"keep the Sabbath,\" or \"broke the Sabbath.\" It was very bad if someone did not keep the Sabbath.</p><p>The Sabbath was a day of rest for everyone in a family, even for <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"eng-t123-v3\" data-resource-code=\"FIAKeyTerms\">servants</data> or <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"eng-t126-v3\" data-resource-code=\"FIAKeyTerms\">slaves</data>, and for the animals as well. It was a day of rest for the whole creation that God had made.</p><p>God also told the Israelites to allow the land to have a Sabbath rest. For six years, the Israelites could plant their fields, take care of their fruit trees, and harvest their crops. During the next year, the seventh year, the Israelites must not plant, take care of plants, or gather together to harvest crops like the Israelites did during the other six years. Instead, the people were supposed to eat whatever the people could find from whatever was growing on its own. Even without the Israelite people actively planting and harvesting, the plants would still grow things to eat. This helped the Israelites remember that God was the one who really owned the land of Israel and provided food for the Israelites. This rest also helped the land stay healthy, so that the people did not destroy the good soil by farming too much. Every 50 years, the Israelites would have a special rest year, which God called the <data class=\"resource-ref\" data-content-id=\"eng-t276-v1\" data-resource-code=\"FIAKeyTerms\">Year of Jubilee</data>.</p><p>By the time of Jesus, the Jewish religious teachers were so careful not to disobey God that they had made many extra rules about the Sabbath day. These rules were very difficult to follow. The religious leaders had forgotten that the day was meant to rest and to honor God. Jesus and the religious leaders had several arguments about the Sabbath. Jesus was often frustrated with the religious leaders because they thought the rules people made were more important than the good law that God had given, and they did not think about what God really wanted.</p>",
